{
  "hypothesis": "The current policy ranks purely by failure_rate, which repeatedly selects memory (highest failure_rate) despite its recent gain, starving exploration of other modules with real defects (recovery, prompt, tools). A composite score that combines failure_rate with stagnation and a small exploration bonus for unattempted modules will diversify proposals and surface remaining real defects, while still prioritizing high-failure modules.",
  "policy": {
    "score": {
      "op": "add",
      "args": [
        {"var": "failure_rate"},
        {
          "op": "add",
          "args": [
            {"op": "mul", "args": [0.5, {"var": "stagnation"}]},
            {"op": "mul", "args": [0.3, {"op": "sub", "args": [1, {"var": "attempted"}]}]}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "branches": 2
  },
  "predicted_decision_change": "Memory (failure_rate 0.83, attempted 0, stagnation 0) still scores highest (~1.13), but recovery (0.67 + 0.3 = 0.97) and prompt (0.5 + 0.3 = 0.8) now outrank tools (0.33 + 0.3 = 0.63) and are competitive with memory, so the second branch will more often target recovery/prompt instead of repeatedly re-proposing memory, increasing coverage of remaining real defects while preserving exploitation of the top failure module."
}

